1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,299
The Umbrella Congress was Tomás's idea. Everything
big that year was Tomás's idea, or

2
00:00:14,319 --> 00:00:15,450
looked like it.

3
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:22,130
He was thirty-three now, and his title was no longer
head of digital. It was chief narrative

4
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:27,793
officer — a title he had invented, and that three
other companies had copied within a

5
00:00:27,813 --> 00:00:33,457
year. He ran a department of fourteen hundred
people across four continents whose job

6
00:00:33,477 --> 00:00:39,120
was to decide what the world felt about Umbrella,
and then to make the world feel it.

7
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:44,917
He had a corner office on the tenth floor and a
house on the Brandywine and his student

8
00:00:44,937 --> 00:00:47,582
loans had been paid off for eight years.

9
00:00:48,152 --> 00:00:53,574
He had also not slept properly since the ghost
video. He did not tell anyone that.

10
00:00:55,645 --> 00:01:02,072
The idea was simple. Once a year, Umbrella would bring
its people together — not the executives,

11
00:01:02,092 --> 00:01:07,847
not the board, not the investors, but the people.
The chemists and the drivers and the

12
00:01:07,867 --> 00:01:13,824
nurses from the foundation clinics and the security
officers and the families. From every

13
00:01:13,844 --> 00:01:18,996
country Umbrella worked in. In one place, on
one night, to hear the Director.

14
00:01:19,566 --> 00:01:25,387
Tomás booked the football stadium in South
Philadelphia for the eleventh of September,

15
00:01:25,407 --> 00:01:31,500
twenty thirty-five. Sixty-eight thousand seats.
Umbrella flew in twelve thousand employees

16
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:37,477
from abroad, and chartered buses for thirty thousand
from the eastern seaboard, and gave

17
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:40,941
free tickets to every family
member who wanted one.

18
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:42,546
They filled it.

19
00:01:44,617 --> 00:01:50,069
They came in the late afternoon, in their tens of
thousands, under a clear September sky.

20
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:55,602
They came in white coats, some of them, because it was
a joke that had become a tradition.

21
00:01:55,622 --> 00:02:01,074
They came with their children on their shoulders.
They came in red-and-white scarves that

22
00:02:01,094 --> 00:02:06,362
Tomás's department had sold at cost, and they waved
them, and filmed each other waving

23
00:02:06,382 --> 00:02:11,895
them, and posted the films, and the films were
watched, that night, by two hundred million

24
00:02:11,915 --> 00:02:12,325
people.

25
00:02:12,896 --> 00:02:18,324
They were happy. That is important. They were not
soldiers or zealots or a mob. They were

26
00:02:18,344 --> 00:02:23,527
forty-one thousand people who worked for the best
employer in the world, and nineteen

27
00:02:23,547 --> 00:02:28,729
thousand people who loved them, and they had been
given free tickets to a concert and

28
00:02:28,749 --> 00:02:33,564
a speech and a light show, and it was a
beautiful evening, and they were happy.

29
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:36,065
That is always how it starts.

30
00:02:38,137 --> 00:02:43,961
In a glass box above the fifty-yard line, Catherine
Adeyemi sat with a glass of wine she

31
00:02:43,981 --> 00:02:49,607
did not drink, and looked down at the crowd, and
thought about a phone in her handbag

32
00:02:49,627 --> 00:02:51,599
that she had bought with cash.

33
00:02:52,169 --> 00:02:58,626
In the press box, among forty journalists who had been
given lanyards and free food, Hannah

34
00:02:58,646 --> 00:03:04,747
Price sat with her laptop closed and a small, worn
spiral notebook open on the desk in

35
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:07,380
front of her, to a page near the end.

36
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:13,998
And in the parking lot outside, among the chartered
buses, a man in a plain jacket stood

37
00:03:14,018 --> 00:03:20,202
by himself, with two private investigators from Oslo,
and listened to the stadium breathe.

38
00:03:22,772 --> 00:03:26,644
At eight o'clock the lights went down,
and the drones went up.

39
00:03:27,215 --> 00:03:33,513
Three thousand of them, built by the factory in
Arlington that had once belonged to Aegis

40
00:03:33,533 --> 00:03:40,115
Dynamics, rising out of the dark behind the stadium in
a single silent cloud, and assembling,

41
00:03:40,135 --> 00:03:46,149
a thousand feet above sixty thousand upturned
faces, into an umbrella. Red and white.

42
00:03:46,169 --> 00:03:50,763
Eight segments. Turning slowly, like a
thing seen from far above.

43
00:03:51,334 --> 00:03:55,814
The stadium roared. It was the first time.
It was only the beginning.

44
00:03:57,885 --> 00:04:03,361
And then, without anyone telling them to — without an
announcement, without a cue — they

45
00:04:03,381 --> 00:04:04,360
began to say it.

46
00:04:04,931 --> 00:04:10,568
It started in the section where the Singapore
families were sitting, and spread, the way

47
00:04:10,588 --> 00:04:16,098
a wave spreads in a stadium, from section to
section, until sixty thousand people were

48
00:04:16,118 --> 00:04:19,956
standing in the dark with their right
hands on their chests.

49
00:04:20,526 --> 00:04:26,331
We are the shelter. We do not wait for the storm. We
make the world safe for those who

50
00:04:26,351 --> 00:04:32,089
cannot make it safe for themselves. What we learn,
we keep. What we begin, we finish.

51
00:04:32,109 --> 00:04:36,899
Where we stand, the rain does not fall. We
are not many. We are enough.

52
00:04:38,269 --> 00:04:39,648
We are Umbrella.

53
00:04:42,718 --> 00:04:45,090
He walked out into the silence afterwards.

54
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:52,037
Alone. No music. No introduction. A single spotlight
on a single microphone in the centre

55
00:04:52,057 --> 00:04:58,147
of a stage the size of a city block, and a man in a
black suit and sunglasses walking

56
00:04:58,167 --> 00:05:03,539
toward it, without hurrying, while sixty
thousand people held their breath.

57
00:05:04,108 --> 00:05:09,829
He had planned to speak for five minutes. It was in
Tomás's schedule. Five minutes, then

58
00:05:09,849 --> 00:05:13,091
the orchestra, then the fireworks,
then the buses.

59
00:05:15,281 --> 00:05:16,157
Good evening.

60
00:05:18,177 --> 00:05:24,134
The roar went on for forty seconds. He let it. He
stood perfectly still at the microphone

61
00:05:24,154 --> 00:05:30,314
with his hands at his sides, and let it wash over him
and break and die away, and he waited,

62
00:05:30,334 --> 00:05:34,613
until the silence came back, and then he
waited a little longer.

63
00:05:35,182 --> 00:05:41,070
Eleven seconds. Somebody in the crowd counted it out
loud. Then others did. By the ninth

64
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:47,044
second, the whole stadium was counting, laughing, and
at nine they cheered again, because

65
00:05:47,064 --> 00:05:51,206
they had all seen the video, and they
were all in on the joke.

66
00:05:51,776 --> 00:05:56,663
He smiled. He did not usually. The screens
showed it, forty feet high.

67
00:05:58,853 --> 00:06:04,715
Nine years ago, in a building two hours down the
road from here, there were six people.

68
00:06:04,735 --> 00:06:10,597
Six. In a building nobody visited, in a company that
was dying, doing work nobody read.

69
00:06:10,997 --> 00:06:16,720
I told them something, in a break room, on a Friday
afternoon. I told them that every

70
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:22,599
great thing that has ever happened in this world
began in a room exactly like that one.

71
00:06:22,619 --> 00:06:24,424
A room nobody was watching.

72
00:06:26,024 --> 00:06:27,040
Look around you.

73
00:06:30,059 --> 00:06:35,275
They looked around. Sixty thousand people, in the
dark, looked at each other. And the

74
00:06:35,295 --> 00:06:40,696
Director felt it — the new sense, the one he had not
yet bought, the one that the System

75
00:06:40,716 --> 00:06:45,686
would sell him that night — like the deck of a
great ship tilting under his feet.

76
00:06:47,877 --> 00:06:53,087
Nobody is watching this room. Not the governments.
Not the newspapers. Not the men in

77
00:06:53,107 --> 00:06:58,563
glass towers who think they run the world. They are
watching the drones, and the numbers,

78
00:06:58,583 --> 00:07:01,456
and the share price. They are
not watching you.

79
00:07:01,855 --> 00:07:02,780
They should be.

80
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:06,783
That was where it changed.

81
00:07:07,353 --> 00:07:13,516
Tomás, in the production booth at the top of the
north stand, would try for years to find

82
00:07:13,536 --> 00:07:19,839
the exact second on the recording, and would never be
sure. But somewhere in that sentence,

83
00:07:19,859 --> 00:07:26,023
the rhythm changed. The voice did not get louder. It
got — the only word Tomás ever found

84
00:07:26,043 --> 00:07:27,343
for it was — older.

85
00:07:29,033 --> 00:07:33,124
Outside these walls, the world is a storm. You know
it. You feel it every morning when

86
00:07:33,144 --> 00:07:37,330
you read the news. Governments that cannot build a
bridge in ten years. Parliaments that

87
00:07:37,350 --> 00:07:41,584
argue while the water rises. Leaders who promise and
forget and promise again. Every year

88
00:07:41,604 --> 00:07:45,647
the storm is worse, and every year the men who are
supposed to hold the umbrella hold

89
00:07:45,667 --> 00:07:49,088
it a little lower, and a little lower, until
the rain falls on everyone.

90
00:07:50,488 --> 00:07:51,385
Not on you.

91
00:07:52,786 --> 00:07:54,850
Where we stand — the rain does not fall.

92
00:07:57,370 --> 00:08:02,788
The roar was different this time. Deeper. The
Director could feel it in the stage, in

93
00:08:02,808 --> 00:08:06,819
the soles of his feet, in his chest,
where the Prototype lived.

94
00:08:07,389 --> 00:08:13,228
And inside him — in a room on the fourth floor of
an imaginary brick building, behind

95
00:08:13,248 --> 00:08:19,431
a door with no name — something had stood up, and
walked to the window, and put both hands

96
00:08:19,451 --> 00:08:20,328
on the glass.

97
00:08:22,517 --> 00:08:27,501
They will tell you that what we are building is too
big. They will tell you that no company

98
00:08:27,521 --> 00:08:32,395
should be this strong. They will tell you to be
afraid of us. And I want you to remember,

99
00:08:32,415 --> 00:08:37,399
when they say it, that they said the same thing about
every good thing that was ever strong

100
00:08:37,419 --> 00:08:38,774
enough to protect anyone.

101
00:08:39,173 --> 00:08:40,256
We are not many.

102
00:08:41,576 --> 00:08:43,990
And sixty thousand people answered him.

103
00:08:44,561 --> 00:08:45,593
We are enough.

104
00:08:48,663 --> 00:08:54,118
It began in the lower bowl, behind the stage. Three
syllables. Somebody shouted them,

105
00:08:54,138 --> 00:08:59,529
and somebody else shouted them back, and then a
whole section, stamping on the beat.

106
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:01,448
Um. Brel. La.

107
00:09:02,018 --> 00:09:08,277
It spread. It went round the stadium like fire
around the rim of a bowl. Um. Brel. La.

108
00:09:08,297 --> 00:09:14,630
Sixty thousand voices, and a hundred and twenty
thousand feet on concrete, so loud that

109
00:09:14,650 --> 00:09:21,202
people in the neighbourhoods of South Philadelphia a
mile away came out onto their porches

110
00:09:21,222 --> 00:09:23,977
to listen. Um. Brel. La. Um. Brel. La.

111
00:09:27,547 --> 00:09:33,045
He did not stop them. He stood at the microphone,
perfectly still, and let it go on.

112
00:09:33,615 --> 00:09:35,420
This was the third threshold.

113
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:42,139
The first had been a parking lot, and four men, and a
dashcam. The second had been a desert,

114
00:09:42,159 --> 00:09:47,840
and twelve men, and a thing that walked through
gunfire. This one had no bodies in it

115
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:53,808
at all. It was only sixty thousand happy people, on a
beautiful September night, chanting

116
00:09:53,828 --> 00:09:55,283
the name of a company.

117
00:09:55,854 --> 00:09:58,232
It was the most dangerous of the three.

118
00:10:00,922 --> 00:10:01,932
Listen.

119
00:10:03,333 --> 00:10:04,934
Listen to them.

120
00:10:07,455 --> 00:10:13,359
The old voice was weeping. There is no other word.
Not with sorrow. With a joy so total

121
00:10:13,379 --> 00:10:19,352
and so old that it had been waiting a hundred years
to be felt again — since beer halls,

122
00:10:19,372 --> 00:10:25,345
and torchlit stadiums, and a crowd that had once
followed it over the edge of the world.

123
00:10:25,914 --> 00:10:31,615
And the Wesker half of the mind, standing at the
microphone with the stadium thundering

124
00:10:31,635 --> 00:10:37,204
around it, felt something else. Something it had
not expected, and would think about,

125
00:10:37,224 --> 00:10:39,045
alone, for a very long time.

126
00:10:39,615 --> 00:10:40,775
It was cold.

127
00:10:42,966 --> 00:10:44,142
It was too easy.

128
00:10:44,660 --> 00:10:52,435
It is always easy. That is what no one ever
understands. It is always, always easy.

129
00:10:52,956 --> 00:10:54,440
That's what frightens me.

130
00:10:58,590 --> 00:11:03,754
Assessment. Crowd, fanaticism.
Belief, ignited.

131
00:11:05,974 --> 00:11:07,277
Level sixty.

132
00:11:09,496 --> 00:11:11,942
New ability. Banner sense.

133
00:11:17,402 --> 00:11:22,011
He bought it standing at the microphone, while
they chanted, and felt it arrive.

134
00:11:22,581 --> 00:11:28,436
The stadium was no longer sixty thousand people. It
was one thing. A single great organism,

135
00:11:28,456 --> 00:11:34,054
breathing, with sixty thousand hearts, and he could
feel every one of them — which were

136
00:11:34,074 --> 00:11:40,059
burning, which were flickering, which were afraid of
the thing they were feeling and chanting

137
00:11:40,079 --> 00:11:45,677
anyway. He could feel a nurse from Lagos in section
two-fourteen who would die for him.

138
00:11:45,697 --> 00:11:51,553
He could feel a chemist from Cambridge in section
one-ten who was chanting because everyone

139
00:11:51,573 --> 00:11:57,106
else was, and would remember it with shame for the
rest of her life. He could feel the

140
00:11:57,126 --> 00:12:02,659
woman in the glass box above the fifty-yard line,
not chanting at all, holding a glass

141
00:12:02,679 --> 00:12:08,147
of wine, and he could feel — like a cold draught
under a door — exactly how close she

142
00:12:08,167 --> 00:12:09,180
was to breaking.

143
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:14,748
The world woke up to the photographs
the next morning.

144
00:12:15,317 --> 00:12:21,265
Every front page on Earth carried the same image: a
stadium in the dark, sixty thousand

145
00:12:21,285 --> 00:12:27,163
fists in the air, and three thousand drones in the
shape of an umbrella. The headlines

146
00:12:27,183 --> 00:12:32,650
were different in every language. In most of
them, they asked the same question.

147
00:12:33,221 --> 00:12:34,565
Cult, or company?

148
00:12:36,634 --> 00:12:42,278
In the press box, long after the other journalists
had gone, Hannah Price sat alone with

149
00:12:42,298 --> 00:12:47,943
Walter Pruitt's notebook open in front of her. She
had read it a hundred times since the

150
00:12:47,963 --> 00:12:51,226
padded envelope had arrived with
no return address.

151
00:12:51,798 --> 00:12:57,635
She turned to the last page he had written on. There
was room at the bottom, below his

152
00:12:57,655 --> 00:13:03,698
blocky capitals. She took out her own pen, and wrote
beneath them, in her own small hand,

153
00:13:03,718 --> 00:13:06,423
the date, and the place, and four words.

154
00:13:06,991 --> 00:13:08,929
They're saying it like soldiers.

155
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,105
Then she underlined it. Twice.

156
00:13:15,676 --> 00:13:21,767
So ended the conquest of the companies. It had taken
four years. It had cost a great deal

157
00:13:21,787 --> 00:13:27,604
of money, and one man's mind, and a great many
people's peace. And it had made Albert

158
00:13:27,624 --> 00:13:33,509
Wesker the master of the most powerful private
institution in the history of the human

159
00:13:33,529 --> 00:13:33,853
race.

160
00:13:34,423 --> 00:13:37,835
He did not intend to stay private
for very much longer.
